\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Finite Gaussian backgrounds and relabelings}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{To compare two discrete Gaussian models, specify which data a change
of labels must carry. A \newvocab{finite Gaussian background} consists
of a \vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002}
#{(V,E,s,h)}, positive weights #{w:E\to\mathbb R_{>0}}, a mass
#{m>0}, and a nonempty proper retained subset #{R\subsetneq V}.
Put #{U=V\setminus R}; both retained and eliminated carriers are
nonempty. The specified reference structures are the
\vocabk{counting inner products}{fdip-0002} on vertex and edge fields,
the \vocabk{weighted edge inner product}{fdip-0007}, and the
\vocabk{induced Borel Euclidean volume}{fdip-002H} #{\lambda_V} on
#{\mathbb R^V}. The [orthogonal coordinate split](fdip-002Q)
identifies it with the product of the retained and eliminated volumes.}

\p{Let #{\extd_{\mathbb R}:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^E} be incidence,
#{(\extd_{\mathbb R}f)(e)=f(h(e))-f(s(e))}, and let #{W} multiply edge fields by
#{w}. The derived operators are
##{L=-\extd_{\mathbb R}^\dagger W\extd_{\mathbb R},\qquad Q=m^2I-L>0,}
where #{\extd_{\mathbb R}^\dagger} is the [counting adjoint](fdip-001P).
The \vocabk{action}{fdip-0004} #{\mathcal A_Q(f)=b_V(f,Qf)/2}, finite
positive [normalizer](fdip-002T) #{Z_Q},
\vocabk{Gaussian law}{fdip-0005} #{\mu_Q}, and
[retained marginal](fdip-000E) #{\mu_S} are derived data.
For the 4 \vocabk{split components}{fdip-001T},
#{S=A-BC^{-1}E}; the real orthogonal specialization has
#{E=B^\dagger}; the block #{B:Y\to X} is distinct from incidence.}

\p{An \newvocab{exact relabeling} to a primed background consists of
bijections #{p:V\to V'}, #{q:E\to E'} satisfying
##{\begin{aligned}
s'q&=ps,&h'q&=ph,\\
w'q&=w,&m'&=m,\qquad pR=R'.
\end{aligned}}
It is the all-positive specialization of a
\vocabk{signed endpoint arrow}{fdip-003B}. It preserves the attachment
of a loop even when its incidence is zero. The induced maps are
##{(Pf)(p(v))=f(v),\qquad (Ja)(q(e))=a(e).}
Allowing coherent endpoint reversals gives the larger signed relation;
it does not change what an exact directed relabeling means.}

\p{The preservation of the pairings, operators, volumes and laws is
\ref{fdip-00KC}. The [counting-basis realization](fdip-00KD) also
compares relabeling with positive scaling and general linear transport.
The covariance-parameter Gaussian density in
\citet{Proposition B.55, p. 505}{friedli2017statistical} agrees with
these conventions after the [inverse-precision theorem](fdip-000B)
identifies #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}}. Its change-of-variables argument does
not specify the endpoint, mass or retained-subset data.}
